 function B(y) {
 function C(z) {
 console.log(x + y + z);
 }
 C(3);
 }
 B(2);
}
A(1); // Logs 6 (which is 1 + 2 + 3)
```

In this example, `C` accesses `B`'s `y` and `A`'s `x`. This can be done because:

1. `B` forms a closure including `A` (i.e., `B` can access `A`'s arguments and variables).
2. `C` forms a closure including `B`.
3. Because `C`'s

